1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,110
Every hour a GPU trains a model, it drinks water twice:

2
00:00:04,110 --> 00:00:08,356
at the building that cools it, and at the power plant that feeds it.

3
00:00:08,356 --> 00:00:11,165
In Chennai, that water is already scarce.

4
00:00:11,850 --> 00:00:13,463
Thirst makes it visible.

5
00:00:13,463 --> 00:00:18,013
This dial is Chennai's next 24 hours from today's live forecast.

6
00:00:18,013 --> 00:00:22,724
Longer wedges are thirstier hours; the amber arc is the best window.

7
00:00:22,724 --> 00:00:28,112
Same job, two start times, and with a cooling tower the water is taken twice.

8
00:00:28,850 --> 00:00:30,607
The second lever is speed.

9
00:00:30,607 --> 00:00:36,655
At 70% power the same job takes a little longer but uses noticeably less energy.

10
00:00:36,655 --> 00:00:39,166
That's a real cut, not just a shift.

11
00:00:39,850 --> 00:00:46,198
A student plans tonight's job: two RTX 4090s, six hours of work, done by nine.

12
00:00:46,198 --> 00:00:51,736
In about a second Thirst says when to start, how fast to run and when it finishes,

13
00:00:51,736 --> 00:00:56,215
with one line on why that time, and savings as ranges, each labelled.

14
00:00:56,215 --> 00:01:02,280
Read the next 48 hours as a timetable, hour by hour, on a calendar, or as a chart.

15
00:01:05,350 --> 00:01:08,088
Then act on it: copy the exact command,

16
00:01:08,088 --> 00:01:14,873
add the run to your calendar with a reminder, or send a link that reopens the same job for your labmate.

17
00:01:16,850 --> 00:01:20,360
Or compare cities: the same job in six Indian cities,

18
00:01:20,360 --> 00:01:25,124
with each one's best start, the next two days, and the water it would take.

19
00:01:26,850 --> 00:01:29,773
On the GPU machine, it's one package.

20
00:01:33,350 --> 00:01:38,343
thirst run waits for the right hour, lowers the GPU power limit,

21
00:01:38,343 --> 00:01:45,044
runs your script, and always puts the limit back, even on Ctrl-C or a dropped SSH session.

22
00:01:45,044 --> 00:01:46,530
Then a signed receipt.

23
00:01:46,530 --> 00:01:51,392
On this machine the energy is modelled; on a lab GPU it's measured.

24
00:01:52,850 --> 00:01:58,031
Receipts add up to a score: of the saving that was possible, how much did you take?

25
00:01:58,031 --> 00:02:03,239
We score the choice, not the climate, and labs compete in weight classes,

26
00:02:03,239 --> 00:02:06,801
so a four-GPU college lab never faces a data centre.

27
00:02:06,801 --> 00:02:09,225
Demo labs are marked simulated.

28
00:02:10,350 --> 00:02:12,905
It all runs serverless on AWS.

29
00:02:12,905 --> 00:02:14,762
Amplify hosts the site.

30
00:02:14,762 --> 00:02:19,059
API Gateway and AWS Lambda run the planner and scoring.

31
00:02:19,059 --> 00:02:23,214
Amazon DynamoDB keeps the weather, plans and receipts.

32
00:02:23,214 --> 00:02:26,997
EventBridge Scheduler refreshes the forecast every hour.

33
00:02:26,997 --> 00:02:31,526
An explainer on Amazon Bedrock with the Strands Agents SDK

34
00:02:31,526 --> 00:02:34,286
is wired in, waiting on model access.

35
00:02:34,286 --> 00:02:39,200
AWS SAM deploys it all from GitHub, with no stored keys.

36
00:02:39,850 --> 00:02:44,523
And every number wears a label: measured, modelled, sourced, or placeholder.

37
00:02:44,523 --> 00:02:48,606
Today our power-cap curve is still a placeholder, and we say so.

38
00:02:49,350 --> 00:02:53,233
Thirst. Run at the right hour, and the right speed, and prove it.

